Four ways to ask for a column

class(trial$age)

[1] "numeric"

class(trial[["age"]])

[1] "numeric"

class(trial[, "age"])

[1] "numeric"

class(trial["age"])

[1] "data.frame"




Three hand you a vector. The fourth hands you a one-column data frame — [ with a single argument is list indexing, and [ on a list returns a list.

The drop surprise

class(trial[, c("age", "sbp_0")])

[1] "data.frame"

class(trial[, "age"])

[1] "numeric"



Same notation, different class, because the result had one column.



class(trial[, "age", drop = FALSE])

[1] "data.frame"

The same answer using dplyr

tidy_summary &lt;- trial |&gt;
  filter(age &gt;= 60, !is.na(sbp_12)) |&gt;
  mutate(change = sbp_12 - sbp_0) |&gt;
  group_by(arm) |&gt;
  summarise(mean_change = mean(change), .groups = "drop")
tidy_summary

