Use AI as translation support, not authority
Copilot is now permitted under the Session 9 policy. When translating:

state the intended R semantics;
inspect indexing and missing-value defaults;
compare row counts and one numerical result;
disclose substantive assistance.

Do not ask AI to translate restricted data or confidential source.

Check it where you know the answer
The bootstrap is most trustworthy when you have first watched it reproduce a result you can verify.

mean_diff &lt;- function(data) {
  mean(data$sbp_12[data$arm == "Treatment"]) -
    mean(data$sbp_12[data$arm == "Placebo"])
}

Bootstrap stability
More resamples reduce computational variation; they do not add participants or repair an inappropriate bootstrap scheme.

boot_interval &lt;- function(seed, B = 1000) {
  set.seed(seed)
  groups &lt;- split(seq_len(nrow(trial)), trial$arm)
  ans &lt;- replicate(B, {
    i &lt;- unlist(lapply(groups, function(j)
      j[sample.int(length(j), replace = TRUE)]))
    mean_diff(trial[i, ])
  })
  unname(quantile(ans, c(.025, .975)))
}

Practice: preserve the assignment mechanism
shuffled &lt;- trial$arm
for (i in split(seq_len(nrow(trial)), trial$site)) {
  shuffled[i] &lt;- trial$arm[i][sample.int(length(i))]
}
Within-site assignment counts stay fixed. Use this rule only when it represents the actual randomization; an arbitrary blocking variable does not establish validity.

Preprocessing belongs inside the loop

cv_predict &lt;- function(data, fold) {
  pred &lt;- rep(NA_real_, nrow(data))
  for (k in sort(unique(fold))) {
    test &lt;- fold == k
    train &lt;- data[!test, ]; assess &lt;- data[test, ]
    m &lt;- mean(train$sbp_0, na.rm = TRUE)
    if (!is.finite(m)) stop("training baseline entirely missing")
    train$sbp_0[is.na(train$sbp_0)] &lt;- m
    assess$sbp_0[is.na(assess$sbp_0)] &lt;- m
    fit &lt;- lm(sbp_12 ~ sbp_0 + age + arm, data = train)
    pred[test] &lt;- predict(fit, newdata = assess)
  }
  pred
}

The same principle applies to scaling, feature selection, and tuning.

Stratified folds

make_folds &lt;- function(arm, k = 5) {
  fold &lt;- integer(length(arm))
  for (i in split(seq_along(arm), arm)) {
    fold[i] &lt;- sample(rep(seq_len(k), length.out = length(i)))
  }
  fold
}

