Software
The primary course environment consists of:

R;
RStudio during the foundational portion of the course;
R Markdown and Quarto for reproducible reports and presentations;
Git and GitHub for version control and submission workflow;
selected tidyverse packages.

The course provides optional exposure to Positron, GitHub Copilot, Python, NumPy, pandas, and notebook-based computing. Students may choose to use these tools when permitted, but they are not required for homework and will not be tested on examinations. Students may continue to use RStudio, R Markdown, and Quarto for their course work.
Course examples use synthetic or unrestricted teaching data. Students must not enter protected, identifiable, or restricted data into course repositories or public AI services.

Base R and tidyverse policy
The course treats base R and tidyverse as two useful approaches within the same R language. Students are not required to adopt one dialect exclusively.
Unless an exercise explicitly targets a particular method, students may choose the approach that makes the analysis easiest to read and verify. Evaluation will focus on:

correctness of the result;
clarity of the data and statistical meaning;
explicit handling of missing values and edge cases;
validation of important assumptions and intermediate results; and
reproducibility and readability.

Students should nevertheless learn to read both styles and occasionally verify an important result using a second approach.

Keep a failed replicate visible

analyse_checked &lt;- function(data, pooled = FALSE) {
  tryCatch({
    value &lt;- analyse_trial(data, pooled)
    if (any(!is.finite(value))) stop("nonfinite result")
    list(value = value, error = NA_character_)
  }, error = function(e) list(value = NULL, error = conditionMessage(e)))
}
bad_data &lt;- data.frame(arm = rep(c("ctrl", "trt"), each = 3), y = 1)
analyse_checked(bad_data)$error

[1] "data are essentially constant"


Retain the replicate ID, scenario, method, and reason. Warnings also need review; this wrapper catches errors, not warnings.

A skewed error with known mean and SD

draw_error &lt;- function(n, target_sd, dgm = "normal") {
  if (dgm == "normal") return(rnorm(n, 0, target_sd))
  if (dgm != "skewed") stop("unknown dgm")
  a &lt;- 0.7
  mu &lt;- exp(a^2 / 2)
  sigma &lt;- sqrt((exp(a^2) - 1) * exp(a^2))
  target_sd * (rlnorm(n, 0, a) - mu) / sigma
}

Subtract the theoretical log-normal mean and divide by its theoretical SD. Centering every generated sample by its own mean would remove sampling variation.

Write a negative log-likelihood

counts &lt;- c(0, 1, 2, 1, 4, 3, 2, 0, 5, 2)
poisson_nll &lt;- function(lambda, y) {
  if (length(lambda) != 1L || !is.finite(lambda) || lambda &lt;= 0)
    return(Inf)
  -sum(dpois(y, lambda = lambda, log = TRUE))
}
poisson_nll(2, counts)

[1] 17.9738


For independent Poisson counts, log-probabilities add. The negative sign makes maximum likelihood a minimization problem. These counts share the same exposure.

Five folds stratified by arm

make_folds &lt;- function(arm, k = 5L) {
  stopifnot(!anyNA(arm), length(k) == 1L, k &gt;= 2L,
            k == as.integer(k), all(table(arm) &gt;= k))
  fold &lt;- integer(length(arm))
  for (i in split(seq_along(arm), arm))
    fold[i] &lt;- sample(rep(seq_len(k), length.out = length(i)))
  fold
}

Each participant appears in one assessment fold and four training folds. Stratification balances arm composition; it does not remove dependence.

Write a negative log-likelihood

